::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A fair number cube is rolled. What is the theoretical probability of rolling a 3?
{
=A. 1/6#Correct. One of the six equally likely outcomes is a 3.
~B. 3/6#This counts too many favorable outcomes. Only one side is 3.
~C. 5/6#This is the complement of rolling a 3.
~D. 6/1#This reverses the probability ratio and is greater than 1.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A coin is flipped 30 times and lands tails 16 times. What is the experimental probability of tails?
{
~A. 1/2#This is the theoretical probability for a fair coin, not the observed data.
=B. 16/30#Correct. Experimental probability uses observed tails over total flips.
~C. 14/30#This is the complement count for heads, not tails.
~D. 30/16#This reverses the ratio. Use favorable observed outcomes over total trials.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
In a survey of 80 people, 20 chose option A. What is the relative frequency for option A?
{
~A. 60/80#This is the count that did not choose option A.
~B. 20/60#This compares option A to not option A, but relative frequency uses the total.
=C. 20/80#Correct. Relative frequency is the category count divided by the total.
~D. 80/20#This reverses the ratio and does not represent a probability.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
Which probability is based on observed results?
{
~A. Theoretical probability#Theoretical probability is based on expected outcomes.
~B. Equally likely probability#This describes a model, not observed data.
~C. Sample space probability#A sample space lists outcomes; it is not necessarily observed data.
=D. Experimental probability#Correct. Experimental probability is based on collected or observed results.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A spinner has 4 equal sections. What is the theoretical probability of landing on one chosen section?
{
=A. 1/4#Correct. One favorable section out of four equal sections gives 1/4.
~B. 4/1#This reverses the probability ratio.
~C. 3/4#This is the complement of one chosen section.
~D. 2/4#This would represent two favorable sections, not one.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
In 25 trials, an event happened 10 times. What is the experimental probability?
{
~A. 15/25#This is the complement count, not the event count.
=B. 10/25#Correct. Use observed successes over total trials.
~C. 25/10#This reverses the ratio.
~D. 10/15#This compares successes to failures instead of total trials.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
What is a marginal total in a two-way table?
{
~A. A count inside the body of the table#That is a joint frequency.
~B. A label at the top of a column#A label names a category; it is not a total.
=C. A row total, column total, or grand total#Correct. Marginal totals appear on the edges of a two-way table.
~D. A probability written as a percent#A percent can represent relative frequency, but it is not a table total.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A survey has 12 students who prefer morning tutoring and 18 who prefer afternoon tutoring. What is the relative frequency for morning tutoring?
{
~A. 18/30#This is the afternoon group, not morning.
~B. 12/18#This compares morning to afternoon instead of the total.
~C. 30/12#This reverses the part-to-total ratio.
=D. 12/30#Correct. There are 12 morning choices out of 30 total students.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
Which statement best compares theoretical and experimental probability?
{
=A. Theoretical uses expected outcomes; experimental uses observed data.#Correct. Theoretical probability comes from a model, while experimental probability comes from results.
~B. Theoretical uses observed data; experimental uses expected outcomes.#This reverses the meanings.
~C. Both always have to be exactly the same.#They can be different, especially with small samples.
~D. Experimental probability cannot be written as a fraction.#Experimental probability is often written as part over total.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A fair coin is flipped 10 times and lands heads 7 times. Which is true?
{
~A. The theoretical probability of heads is 7/10#This uses observed data, so it is experimental.
=B. The experimental probability of heads is 7/10#Correct. It is based on the actual 7 heads in 10 flips.
~C. The theoretical probability of heads is 7/3#This does not match the fair coin model.
~D. The experimental probability of heads is 1/2#This is the fair coin theoretical probability, not the observed result.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
In a two-way table, a cell inside the table shows 9 students who are seniors and prefer online review. What does 9 represent?
{
~A. A grand total#A grand total counts all students in the table.
~B. A column total#A column total counts an entire column.
=C. A joint frequency#Correct. The count fits two categories at the same time.
~D. A theoretical probability#The number is a data count, not a model probability.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
If 45 out of 100 surveyed people choose text reminders, what is the relative frequency as a percent?
{
~A. 4.5%#This moves the decimal too far. 45/100 equals 45%.
~B. 55%#This is the complement percent.
~C. 100%#This would mean everyone chose text reminders.
=D. 45%#Correct. 45 out of 100 is 45%.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A theoretical probability is most appropriate when:
{
=A. outcomes are equally likely and the probability comes from the model.#Correct. Theoretical probability is based on the structure of the situation.
~B. only survey results are given.#Survey results lead to experimental or empirical probability.
~C. the sample total is missing.#A probability cannot be found reliably without needed totals.
~D. the observed results are different every time.#Changing observed results describe experiments, not the theoretical model.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
In 60 trials, an event did not happen 18 times. What is the experimental probability that it did happen?
{
~A. 18/60#This is the probability that it did not happen.
=B. 42/60#Correct. If it did not happen 18 times, it happened 60 - 18 = 42 times.
~C. 60/42#This reverses the ratio.
~D. 18/42#This compares failures to successes, not successes to total trials.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A table shows 24 students in Grade 11 and 26 students in Grade 12. What is the grand total?
{
~A. 24#This is only the Grade 11 total.
~B. 26#This is only the Grade 12 total.
=C. 50#Correct. The grand total is 24 + 26 = 50 students.
~D. 2#This counts grade categories, not students.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
Which ratio represents relative frequency?
{
~A. total / part#This reverses the relationship.
~B. part + total#Relative frequency compares using division, not addition.
~C. total - part#This may give a complement count, but not a relative frequency.
=D. part / total#Correct. Relative frequency is a part-to-total comparison.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A spinner has 5 equal sections. What is the theoretical probability of landing on one specific section?
{
=A. 1/5#Correct. One favorable section out of five equal sections gives 1/5.
~B. 5/1#This reverses the probability ratio.
~C. 4/5#This is the complement of landing on the specific section.
~D. 2/5#This would represent two favorable sections, not one.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
A student makes 18 free throws out of 30 attempts. What is the experimental probability of making a free throw?
{
~A. 12/30#This is the number of missed attempts.
=B. 18/30#Correct. Use made free throws over total attempts.
~C. 30/18#This reverses the ratio.
~D. 18/12#This compares makes to misses, not total attempts.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
In a survey of 40 students, 25 prefer videos. What relative frequency represents students who do not prefer videos?
{
~A. 25/40#This represents students who prefer videos.
~B. 40/15#This reverses the needed ratio.
=C. 15/40#Correct. If 25 prefer videos, then 15 do not, out of 40 total.
~D. 25/15#This compares the two groups but not to the total.
}


::MCR_U05_L02_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U05_L02_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.PR.3
Question:
Why might experimental probability differ from theoretical probability?
{
~A. Experimental probability never uses data#Experimental probability is based on data.
~B. Theoretical probability changes after every trial#The theoretical model does not change because of one trial.
~C. Relative frequency cannot be calculated#Relative frequency can be calculated from data.
=D. Observed results may vary from the expected model#Correct. Actual data can differ from expected probability, especially with fewer trials.
}

